# Logging

Apply in Java (Quarkus) and Python services. Keep logs queryable, correlated, and free of noise or secrets.

## Correlation

- Every request/job carries a correlation id (prefer OpenTelemetry `traceId`; otherwise a request id) and, when known, `userId`.
- Put both in request/job context (Java: MDC; Python: contextvars / structured logger bind) so every log line inherits them — never repeat them in the message.
- Propagate across async work (Mutiny/Vert.x, asyncio tasks) and outbound HTTP.
- Echo the correlation id to clients (e.g. `X-Request-Id`). Scheduled/system work: set `userId=system` (and start a span/trace when available).

## Levels

- **INFO**: normal milestones — use case start (key ids/fields), success, outbound provider call, job start/complete. Default app log level is INFO (no DEBUG in production paths).
- **WARN**: expected business errors / degraded cases (not-found, conflict, invalid request, provider error payload, retries).
- **ERROR**: unexpected failures only, with the exception attached, logged once at the handling boundary (global exception mapper / top-level handler). Never swallow async failures silently.

## Style & content

- Concise action + `key=value` context: `Creating transaction ticker=AAPL type=BUY quantity=10`.
- Log ids, tickers, dates, counts — never secrets, tokens, or full payloads.
- Prefer framework access/request logs for HTTP entry/exit; keep controllers thin (WARN on business→4xx only).

## Where to log

- **Use cases / application services**: INFO at start; INFO/WARN on meaningful outcomes.
- **Outbound adapters**: INFO per external call essentials; WARN on provider errors; ERROR when translation/infra fails.
- **Persistence**: no per-query INFO; let failures surface at the boundary handler.
- **Global unexpected-error handler**: ERROR + stack/exception once; return a safe 5xx body that includes the correlation id.
